  "Question": "If \\(r\\gt0,\\) then for all \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) such that \\(pq\\ne0\\) and \\(pr\\gt qr,\\) we have:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(-p\\gt-q\\)",
    "B": "\\(-p\\gt q\\)",
    "C": "\\(1\\gt-\\frac{q}{p}\\)",
    "D": "\\(1\\lt \\frac{q}{p}\\)",
    "E": "None of these"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "Since \\(r>0\\), dividing \\(pr>qr\\) by \\(r\\) preserves its direction and gives \\(p>q\\). Each proposed conclusion must hold for every allowed pair, so one counterexample rules it out.\n\nTake \\(p=2\\), \\(q=1\\), and \\(r=1\\). These values satisfy both hypotheses, but:\n\n- A claims \\(-2>-1\\), which is false.\n- B claims \\(-2>1\\), which is false.\n- D claims \\(1<\\tfrac12\\), which is false.\n\nFor C, take \\(p=1\\), \\(q=-2\\), and \\(r=1\\). Again the hypotheses hold, but C becomes \\(1>2\\), which is false.\n\nThus none of A through D is guaranteed. The answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
